Electric propulsion systems use electrical energy to accelerate propellant and generate thrust for spacecraft maneuvering. Compared with conventional chemical propulsion, these systems can provide high propellant efficiency, making them suitable for missions that require precise orbital adjustments and extended operational life.

The market primarily covers electric propulsion technologies used in nano- and microsatellites. Applications include orbit raising, station-keeping, attitude control, and other orbital maneuvers. As commercial satellite networks expand, propulsion manufacturers are focusing on compact systems that deliver reliable performance while meeting strict spacecraft power and mass requirements.

